1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,799
Some years just pass by.

2
00:00:01,979 --> 00:00:03,825
Others change everything.

3
00:00:04,005 --> 00:00:07,070
Nineteen sixty-four was one of those years.

4
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:08,940
If you were a kid that year,

5
00:00:08,940 --> 00:00:10,631
you may not have known it at

6
00:00:10,631 --> 00:00:11,174
the time.

7
00:00:11,354 --> 00:00:12,835
But the world you woke up in

8
00:00:12,835 --> 00:00:14,739
on New Year's Day was very different

9
00:00:14,739 --> 00:00:16,432
from the world you went to sleep

10
00:00:16,432 --> 00:00:17,542
in on New Year's Eve.

11
00:00:17,893 --> 00:00:19,758
It started in the shadow of a

12
00:00:19,758 --> 00:00:20,273
tragedy.

13
00:00:20,453 --> 00:00:21,886
Just weeks before,

14
00:00:21,886 --> 00:00:24,752
in November of nineteen sixty-three,

15
00:00:24,752 --> 00:00:28,254
President Kennedy had been killed in Dallas.

16
00:00:28,434 --> 00:00:30,977
The whole country was still in mourning.

17
00:00:31,157 --> 00:00:33,530
Children had watched their parents cry in

18
00:00:33,530 --> 00:00:34,919
front of the television.

19
00:00:35,099 --> 00:00:37,504
Christmas that year had been quiet in

20
00:00:37,504 --> 00:00:38,675
millions of homes.

21
00:00:39,025 --> 00:00:42,214
And then, nineteen sixty-four arrived,

22
00:00:42,214 --> 00:00:44,313
and it turned out to be a

23
00:00:44,313 --> 00:00:45,488
year of music,

24
00:00:45,488 --> 00:00:47,167
laughter, big ideas,

25
00:00:47,167 --> 00:00:48,510
and big changes.

26
00:00:48,690 --> 00:00:50,513
So let's walk through it,

27
00:00:50,513 --> 00:00:51,753
season by season,

28
00:00:51,753 --> 00:00:54,379
through the eyes of an American kid.

29
00:00:54,559 --> 00:00:55,597
If you were there,

30
00:00:55,597 --> 00:00:57,500
see how many of these moments you

31
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:58,019
remember.

32
00:00:58,369 --> 00:00:59,579
On Sunday night,

33
00:00:59,579 --> 00:01:02,301
February ninth, nineteen sixty-four,

34
00:01:02,301 --> 00:01:05,553
families all over America gathered in front

35
00:01:05,553 --> 00:01:08,049
of the television to watch The Ed

36
00:01:08,049 --> 00:01:09,108
Sullivan Show.

37
00:01:09,288 --> 00:01:12,524
That night, Ed Sullivan introduced four young

38
00:01:12,524 --> 00:01:13,890
men from Liverpool,

39
00:01:13,890 --> 00:01:14,466
England.

40
00:01:14,646 --> 00:01:15,609
The Beatles.

41
00:01:15,789 --> 00:01:17,458
And for the next few minutes,

42
00:01:17,458 --> 00:01:19,586
nothing else in the country mattered.

43
00:01:19,936 --> 00:01:24,115
About seventy-three million people watched that broadcast.

44
00:01:24,295 --> 00:01:26,714
That was around four in ten Americans,

45
00:01:26,714 --> 00:01:27,987
in a single evening.

46
00:01:28,167 --> 00:01:30,608
Teenage girls screamed so loudly in the

47
00:01:30,608 --> 00:01:32,923
studio that you could barely hear the

48
00:01:32,923 --> 00:01:33,299
music.

49
00:01:33,479 --> 00:01:35,555
Fathers shook their heads at the long

50
00:01:35,555 --> 00:01:35,836
hair.

51
00:01:36,016 --> 00:01:37,379
And by Monday morning,

52
00:01:37,379 --> 00:01:39,672
every kid at school was talking about

53
00:01:39,672 --> 00:01:39,858
it.

54
00:01:40,209 --> 00:01:42,850
By April, the Beatles held the top

55
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:46,112
five spots on the Billboard singles chart,

56
00:01:46,112 --> 00:01:47,743
all at the same time.

57
00:01:47,923 --> 00:01:50,234
Nobody had ever done that before.

58
00:01:50,414 --> 00:01:52,986
Boys started combing their hair down over

59
00:01:52,986 --> 00:01:53,990
their foreheads.

60
00:01:54,170 --> 00:01:56,770
Guitars sold out in music stores.

61
00:01:56,950 --> 00:02:00,370
And thousands of kids started garage bands,

62
00:02:00,370 --> 00:02:04,586
practicing on Saturday afternoons until the neighbors

63
00:02:04,586 --> 00:02:05,460
complained.

64
00:02:05,811 --> 00:02:07,978
It's hard to overstate how much the

65
00:02:07,978 --> 00:02:10,269
Beatles lifted the country's spirits.

66
00:02:10,449 --> 00:02:12,769
After the darkness of November,

67
00:02:12,769 --> 00:02:15,313
here was something joyful and new.

68
00:02:15,493 --> 00:02:17,624
Many people who were kids at the

69
00:02:17,624 --> 00:02:20,087
time say that February night was when

70
00:02:20,087 --> 00:02:21,751
the sixties really began.

71
00:02:22,102 --> 00:02:23,362
A month earlier,

72
00:02:23,362 --> 00:02:26,040
in January of nineteen sixty-four,

73
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:28,718
a very different kind of news came

74
00:02:28,718 --> 00:02:29,033
out.

75
00:02:29,213 --> 00:02:32,535
Surgeon General Luther Terry released a report

76
00:02:32,535 --> 00:02:35,497
that said, officially and clearly for the

77
00:02:35,497 --> 00:02:38,819
first time, that cigarette smoking caused lung

78
00:02:38,819 --> 00:02:39,325
cancer.

79
00:02:39,505 --> 00:02:41,966
It was front-page news everywhere.

80
00:02:42,317 --> 00:02:43,211
At the time,

81
00:02:43,211 --> 00:02:46,937
about forty-two percent of American adults smoked.

82
00:02:47,117 --> 00:02:49,561
Many kids' parents smoked in the house,

83
00:02:49,561 --> 00:02:51,316
in the car and at the dinner

84
00:02:51,316 --> 00:02:51,692
table.

85
00:02:51,872 --> 00:02:54,507
Ashtrays were on every coffee table.

86
00:02:54,687 --> 00:02:55,941
After the report,

87
00:02:55,941 --> 00:02:57,268
some parents quit,

88
00:02:57,268 --> 00:02:58,300
some tried to,

89
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:00,144
and many just kept going.

90
00:03:00,324 --> 00:03:03,371
But the country's attitude toward smoking had

91
00:03:03,371 --> 00:03:04,455
begun to change,

92
00:03:04,455 --> 00:03:06,013
and it never went back.

93
00:03:06,363 --> 00:03:09,119
In April, the New York World's Fair

94
00:03:09,119 --> 00:03:11,245
opened in Flushing Meadows,

95
00:03:11,245 --> 00:03:11,796
Queens.

96
00:03:11,976 --> 00:03:13,279
Over two seasons,

97
00:03:13,279 --> 00:03:16,190
more than fifty million visitors came.

98
00:03:16,370 --> 00:03:18,254
And if your family took a trip

99
00:03:18,254 --> 00:03:20,515
there, you probably never forgot it.

100
00:03:20,695 --> 00:03:23,027
The symbol of the fair was the

101
00:03:23,027 --> 00:03:25,437
Unisphere, a giant steel globe,

102
00:03:25,437 --> 00:03:26,991
twelve stories high,

103
00:03:26,991 --> 00:03:28,857
surrounded by fountains.

104
00:03:29,207 --> 00:03:31,470
The fair was all about the future.

105
00:03:31,650 --> 00:03:33,704
At the General Motors Futurama,

106
00:03:33,704 --> 00:03:36,354
visitors rode through a vision of cities

107
00:03:36,354 --> 00:03:38,541
under the sea and highways on the

108
00:03:38,541 --> 00:03:38,872
moon.

109
00:03:39,052 --> 00:03:40,956
At the Bell System pavilion,

110
00:03:40,956 --> 00:03:43,269
people could try the Picturephone,

111
00:03:43,269 --> 00:03:45,786
a telephone with a television screen.

112
00:03:45,966 --> 00:03:48,487
Walt Disney built It's a Small World,

113
00:03:48,487 --> 00:03:51,486
where children's dolls from around the world

114
00:03:51,486 --> 00:03:52,781
sang the same song.

115
00:03:53,131 --> 00:03:55,343
Kids who went to the fair remember

116
00:03:55,343 --> 00:03:56,645
the Belgian waffles,

117
00:03:56,645 --> 00:03:58,988
with strawberries and whipped cream,

118
00:03:58,988 --> 00:04:01,656
which many Americans tasted there for the

119
00:04:01,656 --> 00:04:02,697
very first time.

120
00:04:02,877 --> 00:04:05,873
They remember the dinosaurs at the Sinclair

121
00:04:05,873 --> 00:04:08,311
pavilion, and the moving sidewalks.

122
00:04:08,491 --> 00:04:10,361
It felt like the future was right

123
00:04:10,361 --> 00:04:11,381
around the corner,

124
00:04:11,381 --> 00:04:13,251
and it was going to be wonderful.

125
00:04:13,601 --> 00:04:15,742
A day at the fair wasn't cheap

126
00:04:15,742 --> 00:04:16,956
for a big family.

127
00:04:17,136 --> 00:04:19,020
Adult tickets were two dollars,

128
00:04:19,020 --> 00:04:21,269
and children's tickets were a dollar.

129
00:04:21,449 --> 00:04:22,483
Add the drive,

130
00:04:22,483 --> 00:04:25,141
the parking, the food and a souvenir

131
00:04:25,141 --> 00:04:27,135
or two, and a family of six

132
00:04:27,135 --> 00:04:29,868
could spend a week's grocery money in

133
00:04:29,868 --> 00:04:31,271
a single afternoon.

134
00:04:31,451 --> 00:04:33,594
Most parents thought it was worth every

135
00:04:33,594 --> 00:04:33,924
penny.

136
00:04:34,274 --> 00:04:35,267
Also in April,

137
00:04:35,267 --> 00:04:38,318
the Ford Motor Company unveiled a brand-new

138
00:04:38,318 --> 00:04:40,020
car at the World's Fair.

139
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:41,108
It was small,

140
00:04:41,108 --> 00:04:42,645
sporty and affordable,

141
00:04:42,645 --> 00:04:44,600
with a long hood and a short

142
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:44,949
back.

143
00:04:45,129 --> 00:04:46,615
It was called the Mustang.

144
00:04:46,795 --> 00:04:48,990
The base price was about two thousand

145
00:04:48,990 --> 00:04:51,244
three hundred and sixty-eight dollars,

146
00:04:51,244 --> 00:04:53,380
and Ford sold more than four hundred

147
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:55,456
thousand of them in the first year.

148
00:04:55,806 --> 00:04:57,791
The Mustang was made for the young.

149
00:04:57,971 --> 00:04:59,122
For the first time,

150
00:04:59,122 --> 00:05:01,121
a car company was designing a car

151
00:05:01,121 --> 00:05:03,664
specifically for people in their twenties,

152
00:05:03,664 --> 00:05:05,784
and the children right behind them.

153
00:05:05,964 --> 00:05:08,278
Teenage boys pinned pictures of it on

154
00:05:08,278 --> 00:05:09,529
their bedroom walls.

155
00:05:09,709 --> 00:05:12,013
It was the beginning of what people

156
00:05:12,013 --> 00:05:13,923
would soon call the pony car.

157
00:05:14,273 --> 00:05:15,529
Then came the summer,

158
00:05:15,529 --> 00:05:17,084
and it was one of the most

159
00:05:17,084 --> 00:05:19,358
important summers in American history.

160
00:05:19,538 --> 00:05:20,798
On July second,

161
00:05:20,798 --> 00:05:25,505
nineteen sixty-four, President Lyndon Johnson signed the

162
00:05:25,505 --> 00:05:26,933
Civil Rights Act.

163
00:05:27,113 --> 00:05:30,946
It outlawed discrimination based on race,

164
00:05:30,946 --> 00:05:34,873
color, religion, sex or national origin in

165
00:05:34,873 --> 00:05:39,454
jobs, schools and public places like restaurants,

166
00:05:39,454 --> 00:05:41,324
hotels and theaters.

167
00:05:41,674 --> 00:05:43,971
For millions of Black Americans,

168
00:05:43,971 --> 00:05:45,694
especially in the South,

169
00:05:45,694 --> 00:05:47,920
it meant something very simple.

170
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:50,181
The right to sit at a lunch

171
00:05:50,181 --> 00:05:52,107
counter, stay in a hotel,

172
00:05:52,107 --> 00:05:54,959
or use the same restrooms as everyone

173
00:05:54,959 --> 00:05:55,344
else.

174
00:05:55,524 --> 00:05:58,055
Things that most Americans took for granted.

175
00:05:58,235 --> 00:06:01,420
Many people still remember watching the signing

176
00:06:01,420 --> 00:06:02,368
on television.

177
00:06:02,719 --> 00:06:05,609
That summer was also called Freedom Summer.

178
00:06:05,789 --> 00:06:07,717
Hundreds of college students,

179
00:06:07,717 --> 00:06:09,578
many of them from the North,

180
00:06:09,578 --> 00:06:12,636
traveled to Mississippi to help Black citizens

181
00:06:12,636 --> 00:06:13,765
register to vote.

182
00:06:13,945 --> 00:06:15,466
It was dangerous work.

183
00:06:15,646 --> 00:06:18,574
Three young civil rights workers were murdered

184
00:06:18,574 --> 00:06:19,211
that June.

185
00:06:19,391 --> 00:06:20,914
The country was shocked,

186
00:06:20,914 --> 00:06:23,135
and the push for voting rights only

187
00:06:23,135 --> 00:06:24,023
grew stronger.

188
00:06:24,373 --> 00:06:25,399
For most kids,

189
00:06:25,399 --> 00:06:28,773
though, summer of sixty-four was still summer.

190
00:06:28,953 --> 00:06:30,857
School let out in early June.

191
00:06:31,037 --> 00:06:33,786
Days started with cartoons and cereal,

192
00:06:33,786 --> 00:06:36,679
and ended when the streetlights came on.

193
00:06:36,859 --> 00:06:39,537
In between, there were bicycles,

194
00:06:39,537 --> 00:06:41,880
baseball, the swimming pool,

195
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:43,971
and endless games of tag,

196
00:06:43,971 --> 00:06:45,143
hide and seek,

197
00:06:45,143 --> 00:06:46,565
and kick the can.

198
00:06:46,916 --> 00:06:50,284
Family vacations meant piling into the station

199
00:06:50,284 --> 00:06:52,701
wagon, with no seat belts and the

200
00:06:52,701 --> 00:06:55,484
youngest kid lying in the back window.

201
00:06:55,664 --> 00:06:59,336
The interstate highways were still being built,

202
00:06:59,336 --> 00:07:02,070
so many trips took the old two-lane

203
00:07:02,070 --> 00:07:03,555
roads, past diners,

204
00:07:03,555 --> 00:07:06,055
motels and roadside attractions.

205
00:07:06,235 --> 00:07:09,267
Kids counted license plates from other states,

206
00:07:09,267 --> 00:07:11,573
and asked every ten minutes if they

207
00:07:11,573 --> 00:07:12,562
were there yet.

208
00:07:12,912 --> 00:07:15,517
Music came from a transistor radio,

209
00:07:15,517 --> 00:07:18,421
which by sixty-four was small enough to

210
00:07:18,421 --> 00:07:21,027
carry to the beach or the ballpark.

211
00:07:21,207 --> 00:07:23,126
The songs were the Beatles,

212
00:07:23,126 --> 00:07:25,542
of course, but also the Beach Boys

213
00:07:25,542 --> 00:07:27,035
singing I Get Around,

214
00:07:27,035 --> 00:07:29,594
the Supremes with Where Did Our Love

215
00:07:29,594 --> 00:07:31,371
Go, and Roy Orbison's Oh,

216
00:07:31,371 --> 00:07:32,294
Pretty Woman.

217
00:07:32,474 --> 00:07:34,202
A car radio at night,

218
00:07:34,202 --> 00:07:36,012
with the windows down,

219
00:07:36,012 --> 00:07:37,246
was pure magic.

220
00:07:37,597 --> 00:07:38,695
And at the movies,

221
00:07:38,695 --> 00:07:40,098
there was Mary Poppins,

222
00:07:40,098 --> 00:07:42,233
which came out in August and became

223
00:07:42,233 --> 00:07:44,307
one of Disney's biggest hits ever.

224
00:07:44,487 --> 00:07:46,339
There was A Hard Day's Night,

225
00:07:46,339 --> 00:07:47,935
the Beatles' first movie.

226
00:07:48,115 --> 00:07:49,717
And there was Goldfinger,

227
00:07:49,717 --> 00:07:52,088
the James Bond movie that every older

228
00:07:52,088 --> 00:07:53,305
brother had to see.

229
00:07:53,485 --> 00:07:55,157
A ticket was about a dollar.

230
00:07:55,507 --> 00:07:58,388
Nineteen sixty-four was a big year for

231
00:07:58,388 --> 00:07:59,071
toys too.

232
00:07:59,251 --> 00:08:01,886
That was the year Hasbro introduced G.

233
00:08:02,066 --> 00:08:02,577
I.

234
00:08:02,757 --> 00:08:05,463
Joe, which the company was careful to

235
00:08:05,463 --> 00:08:07,072
call an action figure,

236
00:08:07,072 --> 00:08:07,876
not a doll,

237
00:08:07,876 --> 00:08:09,851
so boys would play with it.

238
00:08:10,031 --> 00:08:11,724
He was twelve inches tall,

239
00:08:11,724 --> 00:08:14,134
with movable joints and a little scar

240
00:08:14,134 --> 00:08:15,371
on his right cheek.

241
00:08:15,722 --> 00:08:17,015
Girls had Barbie,

242
00:08:17,015 --> 00:08:19,983
who had come out in nineteen fifty-nine

243
00:08:19,983 --> 00:08:22,037
and was by now a superstar.

244
00:08:22,217 --> 00:08:24,872
Kids also had Etch A Sketch,

245
00:08:24,872 --> 00:08:26,769
Slinky, Silly Putty,

246
00:08:26,769 --> 00:08:28,475
Hula Hoops and Mr.

247
00:08:28,655 --> 00:08:29,723
Potato Head.

248
00:08:29,903 --> 00:08:31,177
And in many homes,

249
00:08:31,177 --> 00:08:33,582
the most exciting thing of all was

250
00:08:33,582 --> 00:08:35,845
the big Sears Christmas catalog,

251
00:08:35,845 --> 00:08:38,038
which showed up in the fall and

252
00:08:38,038 --> 00:08:39,807
got worn out by December.

253
00:08:40,157 --> 00:08:42,873
In September, kids went back to school.

254
00:08:43,053 --> 00:08:45,025
And in nineteen sixty-four,

255
00:08:45,025 --> 00:08:47,070
those schools were bursting.

256
00:08:47,250 --> 00:08:50,228
The baby boom had filled classrooms to

257
00:08:50,228 --> 00:08:51,012
the walls.

258
00:08:51,192 --> 00:08:54,840
Many children sat in classes of thirty-five

259
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:55,604
or forty.

260
00:08:55,784 --> 00:08:58,467
New schools were being built across the

261
00:08:58,467 --> 00:09:01,013
country, and some kids went to school

262
00:09:01,013 --> 00:09:03,765
in shifts, or in portable classrooms out

263
00:09:03,765 --> 00:09:05,072
in the parking lot.

264
00:09:05,422 --> 00:09:08,380
Nineteen sixty-four was also the last year

265
00:09:08,380 --> 00:09:09,578
of the baby boom.

266
00:09:09,758 --> 00:09:12,963
Births in America had peaked around nineteen

267
00:09:12,963 --> 00:09:16,969
fifty-seven, and nineteen sixty-four is usually counted

268
00:09:16,969 --> 00:09:18,280
as the final year.

269
00:09:18,460 --> 00:09:20,964
More than four million babies were born

270
00:09:20,964 --> 00:09:21,606
that year.

271
00:09:21,786 --> 00:09:24,067
Those kids, the youngest boomers,

272
00:09:24,067 --> 00:09:26,209
are now in their early sixties.

273
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:28,812
Television was everywhere by then.

274
00:09:28,992 --> 00:09:30,235
On Thursday nights,

275
00:09:30,235 --> 00:09:31,346
it was Bewitched,

276
00:09:31,346 --> 00:09:32,916
which started that fall.

277
00:09:33,096 --> 00:09:34,359
On Friday nights,

278
00:09:34,359 --> 00:09:35,697
it was Gomer Pyle.

279
00:09:35,877 --> 00:09:38,541
Gilligan's Island debuted in September,

280
00:09:38,541 --> 00:09:41,137
and The Addams Family and The Munsters

281
00:09:41,137 --> 00:09:42,982
both started that fall too.

282
00:09:43,162 --> 00:09:44,587
And on Sunday nights,

283
00:09:44,587 --> 00:09:47,574
of course, the whole family watched Bonanza.

284
00:09:47,924 --> 00:09:50,989
In November, America chose a president.

285
00:09:51,169 --> 00:09:54,656
Lyndon Johnson, who had taken office after

286
00:09:54,656 --> 00:09:58,974
Kennedy's death, ran against Senator Barry Goldwater

287
00:09:58,974 --> 00:09:59,888
of Arizona.

288
00:10:00,068 --> 00:10:02,490
Johnson won by one of the biggest

289
00:10:02,490 --> 00:10:04,105
landslides in history,

290
00:10:04,105 --> 00:10:07,776
carrying forty-four states and more than sixty-one

291
00:10:07,776 --> 00:10:09,832
percent of the popular vote.

292
00:10:10,182 --> 00:10:13,106
Johnson promised what he called the Great

293
00:10:13,106 --> 00:10:14,961
Society, a war on poverty,

294
00:10:14,961 --> 00:10:16,530
more help for schools,

295
00:10:16,530 --> 00:10:19,098
and health care for older Americans.

296
00:10:19,278 --> 00:10:22,109
Many of those promises would become law

297
00:10:22,109 --> 00:10:23,344
in the next year,

298
00:10:23,344 --> 00:10:24,723
including Medicare.

299
00:10:24,903 --> 00:10:27,224
For a kid in nineteen sixty-four,

300
00:10:27,224 --> 00:10:30,881
politics was mostly something grown-ups argued about

301
00:10:30,881 --> 00:10:32,287
at the dinner table.

302
00:10:32,467 --> 00:10:34,534
But it would shape their whole lives.

303
00:10:34,884 --> 00:10:36,886
There was also a cloud on the

304
00:10:36,886 --> 00:10:37,438
horizon.

305
00:10:37,618 --> 00:10:40,550
In August, after a reported clash with

306
00:10:40,550 --> 00:10:43,405
North Vietnamese boats in the Gulf of

307
00:10:43,405 --> 00:10:47,032
Tonkin, Congress passed a resolution giving the

308
00:10:47,032 --> 00:10:49,888
president broad power to use force in

309
00:10:49,888 --> 00:10:50,505
Vietnam.

310
00:10:50,685 --> 00:10:52,728
Most Americans barely noticed.

311
00:10:52,908 --> 00:10:54,399
But within a few years,

312
00:10:54,399 --> 00:10:56,214
many of the boys who were in

313
00:10:56,214 --> 00:10:58,742
junior high in sixty-four would be sent

314
00:10:58,742 --> 00:10:59,131
there.

315
00:10:59,481 --> 00:11:02,186
By December, the country felt different.

316
00:11:02,366 --> 00:11:05,652
Christmas of nineteen sixty-four was brighter than

317
00:11:05,652 --> 00:11:06,638
the one before.

318
00:11:06,818 --> 00:11:10,475
That year, a new television special premiered

319
00:11:10,475 --> 00:11:11,044
on NBC.

320
00:11:11,224 --> 00:11:13,902
It was Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer,

321
00:11:13,902 --> 00:11:17,074
made with stop-motion puppets and narrated by

322
00:11:17,074 --> 00:11:19,258
Burl Ives as a singing snowman.

323
00:11:19,438 --> 00:11:21,795
Kids have watched it every year since.

324
00:11:22,145 --> 00:11:23,020
Under the tree,

325
00:11:23,020 --> 00:11:24,536
there might have been a G.

326
00:11:24,716 --> 00:11:25,041
I.

327
00:11:25,221 --> 00:11:26,605
Joe, a Barbie,

328
00:11:26,605 --> 00:11:27,988
a new bicycle,

329
00:11:27,988 --> 00:11:29,865
or a Beatles album.

330
00:11:30,045 --> 00:11:33,062
Many kids got their first transistor radio

331
00:11:33,062 --> 00:11:34,283
or record player.

332
00:11:34,463 --> 00:11:36,165
And in thousands of homes,

333
00:11:36,165 --> 00:11:37,605
a kid opened a guitar,

334
00:11:37,605 --> 00:11:40,419
and their parents immediately regretted it.

335
00:11:40,769 --> 00:11:43,720
Looking back, nineteen sixty-four was the year

336
00:11:43,720 --> 00:11:45,517
the country turned a corner.

337
00:11:45,697 --> 00:11:48,085
It began in grief and ended in

338
00:11:48,085 --> 00:11:48,483
hope.

339
00:11:48,663 --> 00:11:50,626
The Beatles brought a new sound.

340
00:11:50,806 --> 00:11:53,168
The Civil Rights Act began to tear

341
00:11:53,168 --> 00:11:55,182
down a system of segregation.

342
00:11:55,362 --> 00:11:57,731
The World's Fair showed us the future.

343
00:11:57,911 --> 00:12:00,326
And the youngest baby boomers were born.

344
00:12:00,676 --> 00:12:03,038
It was also the last year before

345
00:12:03,038 --> 00:12:05,180
many of the big storms of the

346
00:12:05,180 --> 00:12:06,214
later sixties.

347
00:12:06,394 --> 00:12:08,480
Before the war in Vietnam grew,

348
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:11,307
before the riots and the assassinations of

349
00:12:11,307 --> 00:12:12,721
nineteen sixty-eight.

350
00:12:12,901 --> 00:12:15,117
For many people who were kids in

351
00:12:15,117 --> 00:12:17,679
sixty-four, it was the last year that

352
00:12:17,679 --> 00:12:20,172
felt simple, and the first year that

353
00:12:20,172 --> 00:12:20,796
felt new.

354
00:12:21,146 --> 00:12:22,958
If you were a kid in nineteen

355
00:12:22,958 --> 00:12:25,395
sixty-four, you were part of something.

356
00:12:25,575 --> 00:12:26,852
You saw the Beatles,

357
00:12:26,852 --> 00:12:28,576
maybe you went to the fair,

358
00:12:28,576 --> 00:12:30,364
and you grew up in a country

359
00:12:30,364 --> 00:12:31,833
that was changing fast.

360
00:12:32,013 --> 00:12:34,747
Those memories belong to a generation that

361
00:12:34,747 --> 00:12:37,156
would go on to change America itself.

362
00:12:37,506 --> 00:12:39,386
So tell us in the comments.

363
00:12:39,566 --> 00:12:42,167
How old were you in nineteen sixty-four,

364
00:12:42,167 --> 00:12:44,118
and what do you remember most?

365
00:12:44,298 --> 00:12:46,079
Did you watch the Beatles on Ed

366
00:12:46,079 --> 00:12:46,596
Sullivan?

367
00:12:46,776 --> 00:12:48,228
Did you go to the World's Fair?

368
00:12:48,408 --> 00:12:49,789
We read every comment.

369
00:12:49,969 --> 00:12:52,079
Next, we're looking at what it was

370
00:12:52,079 --> 00:12:54,128
like to be a teenager in nineteen

371
00:12:54,128 --> 00:12:54,810
fifty-nine.

372
00:12:54,990 --> 00:12:56,719
Thank you for spending this time with

373
00:12:56,719 --> 00:12:56,860
me.
